    scienceGrounding: 81,
    realitySplit: {
      real:
        'A CMOS camera with a fixed lens images the sky; a plate-solving pipeline identifies the star field and returns an attitude quaternion; a multi-star tracker uses Sirius (α CMa, mag −1.46) and Canopus (α Car, mag −0.74) as bright anchors for a fast, low-compute fix when a full solve is unnecessary. Between solves, a gyroscope propagates attitude and the star fix corrects drift. Accuracy is reported as cross-boresight and roll error in arcseconds or arcminutes, plus update rate, acquisition time and limiting magnitude.',
      narrative:
        'Dogon astronomical knowledge — the elaborate Sirius system tradition recorded by Griaule and Dieterlen — is a living cultural tradition and a contested ethnographic record. The strong claim that the Dogon knew of Sirius B’s 50-year orbit before modern astronomy is disputed by anthropologists (van Beek’s restudy found little support) and should be presented as disputed, not as fact. What this project takes from the tradition is the practice of careful, patient sky observation and the cultural importance of Sirius; the attitude solution itself comes entirely from optics, catalogue matching and estimation theory.',
    },
    summary:
      'A working multi-star attitude reference that solves the sky and uses Sirius/Canopus as anchors. No magic and no false history: the Dogon Sirius-B claim is disputed anthropology, presented as disputed; the accuracy numbers come from a CMOS sensor and a plate solver.',
    science:
      'A star tracker estimates attitude by matching detected star centroids in the image against a catalogue (Hipparcos/Tycho), then solving the rotation between camera and inertial frame. The QUEST or TRIAD algorithms give a least-squares attitude from two or more unit vectors, and a Kalman filter fuses gyro rates with star fixes to bridge the gaps between solves. Accuracy follows the centroiding relation: cross-boresight error ≈ (pixel angular size × centroid noise) / √N_stars, so a 1°-per-pixel sensor with 0.1-pixel centroids and 10 stars can reach tens of arcseconds cross-boresight, while roll error is typically several times worse because it depends on the spatial spread of stars. Bright stars help acquisition (a wide field can see Sirius and Canopus simultaneously from mid-southern latitudes) but a two-star fix is degenerate for roll, so you need either more stars or a gyro for the third axis. Practical degradations: lens distortion, chromatic aberration, sensor blooming on Sirius, rolling shutter, and the 8.6 ly/particle reality that Sirius is a binary (Sirius A and the white dwarf Sirius B, orbital period ~50.1 years) — observing Sirius B requires a telescope, not this camera. The Dogon ethnography is cited as cultural context and explicitly marked as disputed.',
