    buildSteps: [
      { title: 'Characterise the camera', detail: 'Measure the pixel scale and field of view by imaging a known star pair at a known separation. Measure read noise, dark current and the centroiding precision on a bright star across exposure times.' },
      { title: 'Bring up plate solving', detail: 'Install a solver (astrometry.net locally or tetra3) and solve a series of test frames across the sky. Record solve success rate, solve time and the residual against the catalogued field centre.' },
      { title: 'Build the attitude estimator', detail: 'Implement TRIAD for the two-star Sirius/Canopus case and QUEST or a least-squares solve for N stars. Output a quaternion with an estimated covariance from the centroid errors.' },
      { title: 'Add gyro fusion', detail: 'Propagate attitude with gyro rates at 100–200 Hz and apply a Kalman update on each star fix. Tune the process and measurement noise from measured data, not guesses.' },
      { title: 'Calibrate the boresight', detail: 'Align the camera to a reference cube or the vehicle frame using a collimator or a distant surveyed target. Verify by rotating the rig through known angles on a rotary table and comparing.' },
      { title: 'Test across the sky', detail: 'Collect 100+ frames at different altitudes and azimuths, day and night, clear and hazy. Compute cross-boresight and roll error distributions and report the 50th and 95th percentiles separately.' },
      { title: 'Stress the hard cases', detail: 'Test with the Moon in frame, with thin cloud, with Sirius near the field edge (blooming) and with the rig slewing (motion blur). Report where the tracker degrades and why.' },
      { title: 'Write the honest report', detail: 'State the achieved accuracy, the field of view, the update rate and the conditions under which the tracker fails. Separate the cultural section clearly and label the Dogon Sirius-B claim as disputed.' },
    ],
    code: {
      language: 'python',
      note: 'TRIAD attitude from two star unit vectors in the camera frame.',
      snippet:
        'import numpy as np\n\ndef triad(v1_body, v2_body, v1_ref, v2_ref):\n    """Attitude from two non-parallel star vectors. R maps reference -> body."""\n    def frame(a, b):\n        x = a / np.linalg.norm(a)\n        z = np.cross(a, b)\n        z /= np.linalg.norm(z)\n        y = np.cross(z, x)\n        return np.column_stack([x, y, z])\n    Fb = frame(np.asarray(v1_body), np.asarray(v2_body))\n    Fr = frame(np.asarray(v1_ref), np.asarray(v2_ref))\n    return Fb @ Fr.T          # rotation matrix, body from reference\n\ndef error_angles(R_est, R_true):\n    R_err = R_est.T @ R_true\n    angle = np.degrees(np.arccos(np.clip((np.trace(R_err) - 1) / 2, -1, 1)))\n    return angle  # single-axis error; decompose for cross-boresight vs roll if needed\n',
    },
