        'A mobile base carries a microphone array, a speaker, an e-ink face and an edge computer that runs on-device speech recognition and diarisation with no network dependency. It records, transcribes, indexes and replays oral history in a local language, stores everything on local encrypted storage, and syncs only when the community explicitly authorises it. Measured: word error rate on the local language against a held-out human transcript, on-device latency, battery life, recording hours per charge, and a consent-audit pass rate.',
      narrative:
        'The griot is a West African oral historian, praise-singer and living archive — a role with real social authority and real responsibilities about what may be told and to whom. That role is the design brief and the ethical constraint: the robot is a memory aid for the community, never a replacement for the griot, and never an owner of the stories. Consent and data sovereignty are not features bolted on; they are the architecture.',
