    summary:
      'An offline speech-driven archivist robot that records and replays oral history in a local language on-device, with consent and data-sovereignty built into the design. No magic: recognition is a small ASR model, memory is encrypted local storage, and the griot tradition supplies the ethics, not the technology.',
    science:
      'On-device ASR is feasible now: quantised models (whisper.cpp, Vosk, or a fine-tuned wav2vec2/Whisper variant) run in real time on a Pi 5 or a Jetson, and Mozilla Common Voice plus community recordings provide fine-tuning data. The honest limitation is data: word error rate for low-resource African languages is far worse than for English, often 2–5× higher, unless hundreds of hours of transcribed audio are collected with community consent. Diarisation (who spoke when) uses speaker embeddings plus clustering; it degrades with overlapping speech and background noise, so a small mic array with beamforming helps. Privacy engineering is the other real discipline: record only after an explicit spoken consent phrase, store encrypted at rest with per-speaker keys, allow deletion, never upload without an explicit per-recording grant, and design the sync so the community holds the master copy. Fine-tuning and evaluation must use a held-out speaker-split test set, not random splits, or the reported WER will be optimistically wrong.',
