    science:
      'Surface EMG from the residual limb carries enough information for a small number of gestures. The standard pipeline bandpass-filters 20–450 Hz, removes mains hum with a notch, and extracts time-domain features (RMS, mean absolute value, waveform length, zero crossings, slope-sign changes) over 100–250 ms windows; a simple LDA or a small neural network separates 3–6 classes with reported accuracies commonly in the 85–95% range on healthy subjects, and lower with electrode shift, sweat and fatigue. Proportional control maps RMS amplitude to grip speed or force. Force feedback closes the loop with an FSR or load cell in the fingertip and a vibrotactile or pressure cuff actuator on the residual limb; the control bandwidth needed is modest (5–20 Hz), because human grasp force modulation is slow. Mechanical design is where low-cost hands fail: the underactuated tendon-driven layout with a compliant TPU fingertip is robust and printable, but the servo torque, tendon friction and pinch force must be balanced, and the socket fit matters more than the electronics for user acceptance. Honest reporting means bench grip force with a calibrated load cell, classifier accuracy on a held-out session (not a random split of the same session), weight, and cost with labour excluded and included.',
